# The consistent dumps of the two LIVE application databases, taken immediately
# before the nightly restic run: NPM's SQLite database and Uptime-Kuma's embedded
# MariaDB. deploy/backup.container pulls this in with Wants=/After=, next to
# postgres-dump.service, which does the same for the BoltMesh cluster.
#
# Why a host unit and not a container (postgres-dump.container is one):
# Uptime-Kuma's MariaDB only listens on a unix socket INSIDE its own container
# (/app/data/run/mariadb.sock), so the dump is `podman exec uptime-kuma
# mariadb-dump` -- and a container cannot exec into a sibling. The NPM half runs
# a throwaway container, but the two are one job: both exist to keep the restic
# set free of warm database files (BACKUP_EXCLUDES in stack.conf), and splitting
# them would let one rot unnoticed.
#
# scripts/db-dump.sh owns the mechanics and the validation: each dump is written
# to a .tmp, checked (SQLite PRAGMA quick_check / MariaDB completion trailer) and
# only then renamed over the previous one, so a failed dump leaves the last good
# copy in place -- the snapshot then holds a stale dump, which scripts/monitor.sh
# fails on, rather than a torn fresh one.
#
# No Wants= on the applications: a backup job must never resurrect a service an
# operator stopped. `podman exec` then fails with a clear message, the dump keeps
# its previous copy, and the monitor reports it.
#
# No [Install]: backup.service activates it (and `./deploy.sh --only db-dump`
# runs it by hand).
[Unit]
Description=Consistent dumps of the live application databases (NPM SQLite, Uptime-Kuma MariaDB)
After=nginx-proxy-manager.service uptime-kuma.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=@REPO_DIR@/scripts/db-dump.sh
# The MariaDB dump is a few seconds on this host; the limit is for a big history
# table, not routine operation, and it must stay under backup.service's own start
# wait.
TimeoutStartSec=600
